{"margin": 3.07, "lean": -3.0, "environment": 8.2, "incumbency": -2.0, "money": -0.13}, "rating_margin": -0.25, "sigma_race": 6.0, "sigma_total": 6.79, "baseline_margin": 0.89, "analyst_adjustment": -1.39, "key_factors": ["Ratings consensus is overwhelmingly Tossup, with a slight tilt toward Huizenga", "Fundamentals favor Republicans: R+3 district lean offset by a strong Democratic environment", "Recent polling is thin and partisan-sponsored; the lone fall poll (D-sponsored GBAO) shows McCann up 1", "Prediction markets price Huizenga modestly ahead (roughly 50-53% Republican)"], "rationale": "The race sits at the center of the tossup band: fundamentals give Huizenga a small edge from the district's R+3 lean and incumbency, while the Democratic-leaning national environment and McCann's fundraising keep it dead even. Markets and most raters tilt narrowly Republican, so the forecast lands just below the tie line with wide uncertainty.", "watch": "A credible independent poll or further movement in the national environment could break the deadlock either way.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Bill Huizenga (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Michigan's 4th District is one of the closest House races of the cycle, with incumbent Republican Bill Huizenga seeking another term against state Senator Sean McCann. The district leans Republican by about three points, but a favorable national climate for Democrats has pulled the contest back to even. Expert raters overwhelmingly call it a Tossup, and prediction markets give Huizenga only a narrow edge.\n\nMcCann has kept pace financially, raising $2.6 million, and has campaigned with Pete Buttigieg in Kalamazoo in the race's final weeks. Huizenga retains the advantages of incumbency and a larger war chest, though outside groups have spent heavily against him. Polling is sparse and mostly partisan-sponsored, with the most recent survey showing McCann up one point. The race is genuinely up for grabs.", "history": [{"date":